 original model has people ask whether a coin explains a sequence better than two kinds of rigged coin: one biased toward a face, and one that tends to repeat or alternate. I added a third rigged coin they also consider, one with a two-flip memory. Its next flip depends on the previous two flips (four contexts, each with its own unknown rule). The three rigged coins are equally likely to begin with, and the scoring adds no new free parameters. Long streaks and short repeating patterns reuse only a few three-flip stretches, so the memory coin explains them well and they look rigged even when the two sequences switch equally often. This targets two critique findings that survive the FDR correction: at equal switch counts, people choose the sequence with the shorter longest run, and the one with more distinct three-flip stretches.\n\n**Name:** `bayes_rigged_coin_with_two_flip_memory`\n\n**Check result:** `OK`. It loaded, had a finite log-probability, met the data contract, finished a 100-draw single-chain fit, and gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −2393.3. That is about 8 nats below the parent's −2385.7, but only the full fit can show whether the change helps. Two warnings came with it:\n- **Convergence not verified:** the check runs one chain, so it can't compute R-hat.\n- **PSIS-LOO unreliable on the smoke fit:** 6.5% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. If the full fit shows the same, the ELPD estimate can't be trusted.\n\nThings the check doesn't cover:\n- **Novelty:** the two-flip memory coin has to shift predictions enough to pass the novelty gate against `iter0_candidate0`.\n- **Fit time:** the smoke fit took 113 s, so the full fit should finish within the 30-minute limit, but I haven't confirmed it.","ttft_ms":2815,"type":"result","duration_ms":413304,"uuid":"bca7d160-8d26-43db-b263-09179986dfe9","ttft_stream_ms":1280,"time_to_request_ms":109,"first_content_frame_ms":1280,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
